WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.380
- [Narrator] Salary ranges have come

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.180
to New York City job postings.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.000
This month a new Salary
transparency law took

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.270
effect that requires most
employers to provide good faith

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.520
salary ranges on job posts.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.310
- The significance of all of this is huge.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.920
Pay information has
been secret for decades

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.200
- [Narrator] But since the
legislation went into effect

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.310
job seekers are finding

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.100
that some companies are
sidestepping the new regulation

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.200
by listing salary ranges so wide

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.840
that they stretch hundreds
of thousands of dollars.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.210
So does this loophole
mean New York City's new

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.803
law is ineffective?

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.950
Pay transparency laws exist

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.950
to different degrees in
some states in the US,

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.590
but Colorado and now
New York City stand out

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.770
in that they require this
information in job postings.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.680
Experts say New York City's
Salary Disclosure Law

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.380
may be especially impactful
because of the size

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.080
of the city's economy and the
number of companies there.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.900
New York City is now the
biggest job market in the US

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.170
to require employers to
list pay ranges and job ads.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.170
- This law has national
implications because

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.140
if a job could conceivably
be done in New York

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.060
employers would have to comply with it.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.250
And that applies to remote roles as well.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.260
And already we're
starting to see companies

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.380
across the US begin to
list pay for remote roles

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.360
wanting to comply with
the New York legislation

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.970
- [Narrator] Officials say
the New York City law helps

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.390
eliminate inherent bias in
setting employee salaries.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.580
Last year, the Pew Research Center found

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.900
that women earned around 84%
of what men earned in 2020.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.970
That gap has narrowed significantly

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.920
from 40 years ago when women earned

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.410
around 64% of what men did.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.510
- Lawmakers and other supporters hope laws

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.060
like this will lead to
more equitable workplaces

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.430
will lead to more fairness in the process

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.130
and will help to sort of
level the playing field really

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.900
from the moment someone gets hired.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.060
- [Narrator] New York's
law did face pushback

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.410
from prominent business groups

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.300
like the partnership for New York City

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.820
which said the legislation
was overly burdensome

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.400
for employers who needed
more time to comply

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.660
with the new rules.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.780
- Ultimately, the city did
give companies more time

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.580
and the city pushed
back the implementation

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.190
of a law from May until
November of this year.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.120
Right now, the partnership

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.010
for New York City said most employers

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.240
are on board with this.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.250
They may not be thrilled about it

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.170
but they're largely complying.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.630
- [Narrator] Since the
transparency law went into effect

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.370
companies have pursued a variety

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.230
of approaches to disclosure.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.320
- Some companies still
aren't complying at all.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.290
Others are posting really broad ranges.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:29.070
Some companies have had ranges
that vary only by $5,000

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.710
and so it's a really mixed picture so far.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.420
- [Narrator] The roles that
have attracted the most

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.270
attention are those
with wide Salary ranges.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.320
A trauma surgeon

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.660
in the Mount Sinai health
system could earn anywhere

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.100
from $384,000 to $800,000 or more.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.210
A tax executive

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:49.200
at Price Waterhouse
Coopers could make $158,000

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.760
or nearly triple that.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.050
A radiologist position

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.780
at another Mount Sinai
Hospital listed a job

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:55.620
that could pay as much

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.333
as $525,000 or roughly half
that amount at $260,000.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:03.630
- One of the really fuzzy parts

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.600
of this law is what
constitutes a good faith range

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.000
because employers have made
the case that sometimes

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.740
ranges need to be super broad

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.410
because they don't know who
they wanna recruit for a role.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.000
Job seekers have said

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.640
that more specific
ranges are more helpful.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.540
- [Narrator] The agency tasked

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.000
with enforcing the law is
New York City's Commission

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.790
on Human Rights, which will rely mostly

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.430
on citizen complaints
to investigate companies

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.830
that post jobs without
the pay range listed.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.870
It's unclear whether the city's Commission

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.420
on Human Rights will
consider the broad Salary

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.480
ranges a good faith
attempt to post salaries.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.020
- One co-sponsor of the
latest legislation told me

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.480
that there may need to
be additional legislation

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.060
soonish to address the fact

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.100
that there are overly broad ranges.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.150
This council member said

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.760
that he would like to see
a mechanism, for example

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.940
that prevents ranges that
are double the minimum

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:54.773
and maximum.

00:03:54.773 --> 00:03:57.870
So example, a range
from 60,000 to 120,000.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.553
He would see as unacceptable.

